verdicts.reason:
This is a real breach, narrowed slightly. The owner lesson gives an absolute rule: map "RO" only for a genuinely read-only view, "never" for a register whose fields are read-only. Three later lessons, two of which link back to the owner, do exactly that in their reference code and say nothing about it. One of them (frontdoor-vs-backdoor) builds its rights lesson on that choice. No fact is wrong: the claims about rights in both places match uvm-core. But the course recommends two incompatible practices for the same add_reg() decision, and the later examples look like the model answer. That makes it S2, not S1.

The narrowing: A-UVM-4A line 1286 ("RW" rights unless the bus really is read-only) and lines 1252/1279 (W1C under "RO" rights) are consistent and need no change. A-UVM-4A line 1766 maps INT_STATUS "RO" on purpose inside a find-the-bug exercise, so it is not a breach. The 4B index dbg_map at line 333 is the sanctioned debug-port case.

Part of the proposed fix is unsafe. The option "change the three examples to RW" would break frontdoor-vs-backdoor, which depends on OVF being mapped "RO":
- line 370: the silent ovf.write backdoor failure;
- line 565: the troubleshooting row recommending poke() for an RO-mapped register;
- lines 1164 and 1179: the mirror walkthrough, "map rights RO, so the read is allowed";
- the decision-flow figure, gate 3.

The other option, softening 4A:353 and annotating line 874, is safe.
